No [1]; I'm saying they violate the licence, unless uploaded by the copyright
holders (who can do what they like, but particularly with the death of
Izchak I doubt there is any set of people who are meaningfully in a state
to make decisions there).

NetHack License, section 2, para b) (which is a condition you must comply
with to distribute NetHack):

    b) cause the whole of any work that you distribute or publish, that in
    whole or in part contains or is a derivative of NetHack or any part
    thereof, to be licensed at no charge to all third parties on terms
    identical to those contained in this License Agreement (except that you
    may choose to grant more extensive warranty protection to some or all
    third parties, at your option)

Any software distributed through the App Store has additional conditions
imposed by the User Agreement, and hence such distribution does not comply
with para b). The effect is that (as with VLC, for example) at any point
any of the Dev Team could complain to the App Store and have NetHack - or
a port of any NetHack derivative such as Slash'Em - pulled from it.
Whether or not they care is another matter, but the NetHack README does
say:

4.  Before you do anything else, please read carefully the file called
    "license" in the 'dat' subdirectory.  It is expected that you comply
    with the terms of that license, and we are very serious about it.

Like I said: buy an uncrippled device next time.

[1] Well, it's not impossible that crazy legislation in some jurisdictions
that tend to roll over to big copyright has made this a criminal matter,
but it isn't necessarily so.
